### Scope and support

All32 lecture pages are accounted for across the three Week3 notes and hub. Title/closing/read-next-week items do not add later academic topics. Bounded textbook consultation used *Introductory Econometrics: A Modern Approach*, Jeffrey M. Wooldridge, 5th ed., §11.1 stationary/weakly dependent processes and moment derivations, §11.2 assumptions/theorems, and relevant §11.3 random-walk/differencing qualifications. The full textbook, the optional 1994 chapter, linked video, Chapter12 methods and formal Chapter18 unit-root testing are not ingested. Nearby citations distinguish these supplementary explanations from supplied lecture material. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Econometrics_II/Week_3/2026_Lecture3_print.pdf#page=1|2026_Lecture3_print.pdf, physical PDF p. 1–3, 19, 30–31]]


## Worked example

O019-W1, item_version 1, worked_example. **Prompt:** A time-series regression has a valid TS1a–3a consistency argument. For one coefficient suppose in a hypothetical large-sample calculation the orthogonal design component has $Q$ diagonal entry $q=4$ (so its inverse entry is $1/q$), error variance $\sigma^2=9$, sample size $n=100$, estimate $\widehat\beta_j=0.5$ and null $\beta_{j,0}=0$. Under the additional stated lecture inference assumptions and applicable score CLT/moments, derive the scaled-normal covariance and ordinary variance approximation, then compute an approximate standard error and standardized statistic. State TS4a and TS5a precisely, contrast weak dependence of data with no relevant serial error covariance, and explain what survives or fails if either inference condition fails. Distinguish asymptotic from exact finite-sample inference, normality and broad efficiency claims; explain why including a trend cannot cure an arbitrary unit root.

**Complete solution and essential rubric:** TS4a requires $\operatorname{Var}(u_t\mid x_t)=\sigma^2$; TS5a requires $E[u_tu_s\mid x_t,x_s]=0$ for distinct dates. They are additional error restrictions, not consequences of stationary or weakly dependent regressors. Multiply the consistency identity by $\sqrt n$; the score CLT plus the convergent inverse design gives limiting covariance $\sigma^2Q^{-1}$ under these restrictions. Thus $\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\beta_j)\approx\sigma^2/(nq)$ in this supplied scalar/orthogonal component illustration; $\operatorname{se}\approx\sqrt{9/(100(4))}=0.15000000$ and $(\widehat\beta_j-\beta_{j,0})/\operatorname{se}=3.33333333$. These are declared large-sample approximations, not an exact finite-sample Student law or actual data estimate. Full estimated variance uses $\widehat\sigma^2(Z\prime Z)^{-1}$ and its applicable assumptions. TS1a–3a consistency may survive heteroskedasticity/serial error correlation, while the usual variance/test guarantee need not. Weak dependence permits some serial dependence in the joint data; TS5a addresses the error products relevant to ordinary standard errors. An error-normality assumption is unnecessary for this asymptotic route; exact finite-sample t/F needs stronger classical conditions. The lecture supplies no estimator class/bound for a universal efficiency claim. A correct deterministic trend can remove that deterministic component, but not accumulated stochastic unit-root shocks; formal robust corrections and unit-root tests remain outside this lesson. All requested calculations, derivations and qualifications are essential; the component map above applies to every full form.

